1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,240
A few words on pronunciation. I'd like to introduce you to the pronunciation guide

2
00:00:05,240 --> 00:00:08,440
outlines in the following chart. There aren't too many characters that are

3
00:00:08,440 --> 00:00:12,020
different from the standard alphabet, but just so you'll be familiar with them, look

4
00:00:12,020 --> 00:00:18,120
at the chart. It shows eight tense vowels and six lax vowels and semi-vowels. Tense

5
00:00:18,120 --> 00:00:24,980
vowels, lax vowels. In some books, tense vowels are called long and lax vowels are

6
00:00:24,980 --> 00:00:29,120
called short. Since you'll be learning how to lengthen vowels when they come before a

7
00:00:29,120 --> 00:00:35,180
voiced consonant, it would be confusing to say that hen has a long, short vowel. It's

8
00:00:35,180 --> 00:00:40,240
more descriptive to say that it has a lax vowel that is doubled or lengthened. Here

9
00:00:40,240 --> 00:00:56,280
are the tense vowels. A, take. E, eat. I, ice. O, hope. O, smooth. ah, caught, ah,

10
00:00:56,880 --> 00:01:11,760
cat, ow, down, here are the lax vowels, eh, get, ih, it, uh, took, uh, some, and

11
00:01:11,760 --> 00:01:20,180
the semi-vowels, ur, hur, ul, dull. Now, although this may look like a lot of

12
00:01:20,180 --> 00:01:28,540
characters to learn, there are really only four new ones, ah, ah, uh, and uh. Under

13
00:01:28,540 --> 00:01:32,500
tense vowels, you'll notice that the vowels that say their own names simply

14
00:01:32,500 --> 00:01:41,180
have a line over them, a-e-i-o-u. There are three other tense vowels. First, ah is

15
00:01:41,180 --> 00:01:45,320
pronounced like the sound you make when the doctor wants to see your throat, or

16
00:01:45,320 --> 00:01:52,420
when you loosen a tight belt and sit down in a soft chair. Ah. Next you'll find ah,

17
00:01:52,920 --> 00:01:59,060
a combination of the tense vowel ah and the lax vowel eh. It is similar to the

18
00:01:59,060 --> 00:02:06,640
noise that a goat or a lamb makes, half, last, chance. The last one is ow, a

19
00:02:06,640 --> 00:02:14,460
combination of ah and oh. This is a very common sound, usually written as O-W or O

20
00:02:14,460 --> 00:02:21,020
-U in words like down or round. A tense vowel requires you to use a lot of facial

21
00:02:21,020 --> 00:02:26,740
muscles to produce it. If you say E, you must stretch your lips back. For OO, you

22
00:02:26,740 --> 00:02:33,200
must round your lips forward. For AH, you drop your jaw down. For AH, you'll drop

23
00:02:33,200 --> 00:02:39,400
your jaw far down and back. For A, bring your lips back and drop your jaw a bit.

24
00:02:39,400 --> 00:02:45,060
For I, drop your jaw for the ah part of the sound and pull it back up for the e

25
00:02:45,060 --> 00:02:51,900
part. And for O, round the lips, drop the jaw, and pull back up into ooh. An

26
00:02:51,900 --> 00:03:03,900
American O is really oh-ooh. Now you try it. Repeat after me. E, ooh, ay, ah, ah,

27
00:03:04,900 --> 00:03:11,960
I, oh. A lax vowel, on the other hand, is very reduced. In fact, you don't need to

28
00:03:11,960 --> 00:03:15,880
move your face at all. You only need to move the back of your tongue and your

29
00:03:15,880 --> 00:03:20,840
throat. These sounds are very different from most other languages. Under lax

30
00:03:20,840 --> 00:03:24,080
vowels, there are four reduced vowel sounds starting with the Greek letter

31
00:03:24,080 --> 00:03:29,840
epsilon, pronounced e, the letter i, pronounced i, and the u with two dots,

32
00:03:30,300 --> 00:03:37,160
pronounced u, which is a combination of i and u, and the schwa, pronounced u. Most

33
00:03:37,160 --> 00:03:42,360
reduced, most relaxed sound that we can produce. It is also the most common sound

34
00:03:42,360 --> 00:03:47,340
in English. The semi-vowels are the American R, pronounced er, which is the

35
00:03:47,340 --> 00:03:53,740
schwa plus R, and the American L, which is the schwa plus L. Voice consonants?

36
00:03:54,100 --> 00:03:59,060
Unvoiced consonants? A consonant is a sound that causes two points of your mouth

37
00:03:59,060 --> 00:04:05,560
to come into contact in three locations. The lips, the tip of the tongue, and the

38
00:04:05,560 --> 00:04:11,680
throat. A consonant can either be unvoiced, whispered, or voiced, spoken,

39
00:04:11,680 --> 00:04:16,560
and it can appear at the beginning, middle, or end of a word. You'll notice

40
00:04:16,560 --> 00:04:22,660
that for some categories, a particular sound doesn't exist in English. parry,

41
00:04:22,900 --> 00:04:36,660
berry, apple, able, mop, mob, ferry, very, afraid, avoid, off, of, stew, zoo, racist,

42
00:04:36,680 --> 00:04:51,080
raises face phase sheet pressure pleasure crush garage to do pedal pedal not nod

43
00:04:51,080 --> 00:05:04,720
choke joke gaucho gouger rich ridge think that ether either tooth Smooth, come, gum,

44
00:05:05,320 --> 00:05:17,240
bicker, bigger, pick, pig, accent, exit, tax, tags, yes, player, day, wool, shower,

45
00:05:17,920 --> 00:05:28,500
now, his, ahead, late, collect, towel, rate, correct, tower, me, swimmer, Same.

46
00:05:29,120 --> 00:05:37,660
Next. Connect. Man. Finger. Ring. Pronunciation points. In many

47
00:05:37,660 --> 00:05:41,980
dictionaries, you may find a character that looks like an upside-down V and

48
00:05:41,980 --> 00:05:47,680
another character that's an upside-down E, the schwa. There's a linguistic

49
00:05:47,680 --> 00:05:53,040
distinction between the two, but they're pronounced exactly the same. Since you

50
00:05:53,040 --> 00:05:56,960
can't hear the difference between these two sounds, we'll just be using the

51
00:05:57,980 --> 00:06:04,540
indicate the schwa sound. It's pronounced uh. The second point is that we don't

52
00:06:04,540 --> 00:06:10,920
differentiate between ah and aw. The a with two dots is pronounced ah. The

53
00:06:10,920 --> 00:06:17,980
backward c is more or less pronounced aw. This aw sound has a back e sound to it,

54
00:06:17,980 --> 00:06:22,500
and as it's not common to the entire United States, it won't be included here.

55
00:06:23,400 --> 00:06:28,560
R can be considered a semivowel. One characteristic of a vowel is that nothing

56
00:06:28,560 --> 00:06:35,200
in the mouth touches anything else. R definitely falls into that category. So in

57
00:06:35,200 --> 00:06:39,460
the exercises throughout the book, it'll be treated not so much as a consonant, but

58
00:06:39,460 --> 00:06:46,180
as a vowel. The OU sound is usually indicated by A with two dots U, which

59
00:06:46,180 --> 00:06:52,800
would be OU. This may have been accurate at some point in some locations, now

60
00:06:52,800 --> 00:07:04,660
generally, ow. Town is town, how is how, loud is loud, and so on. Besides voiced

61
00:07:04,660 --> 00:07:09,280
and unvoiced, there are two words that come up in pronunciation. These are

62
00:07:09,280 --> 00:07:15,640
sibilant and plosive. When you say the S sound, you can feel the air sliding out

63
00:07:15,640 --> 00:07:21,780
over the tip of your tongue. This is a sibilant. When you say the P sound, you

64
00:07:21,780 --> 00:07:27,980
can feel the air popping out from between your lips. This is a plosive. Be aware

65
00:07:27,980 --> 00:07:31,720
that there are two sounds that are sometimes mistakenly taught as sibilants

66
00:07:31,720 --> 00:07:38,560
but are actually plosives, TH and V. However, instead of words like plosive and

67
00:07:38,560 --> 00:07:44,660
sibilant, we'll be using commonly used words like hissed, buzzed, and popped to

68
00:07:44,660 --> 00:07:50,300
refer to consonants. For particular points of pronunciation that pertain to your own

69
00:07:50,300 --> 00:08:04,780
language, refer to the Nationality Guides. Vowel chart, ee-beat, ae-bait, ii-bit, ee

70
00:08:04,780 --> 00:08:09,940
-bet, ow-about, ae-bat.
